Ladybird renders an SVG image that has a viewBox in a viewport with the
proportions of the image’s box, whatever transforms the box is under.
The transforms then stretch what was rendered. So under scale(2, 4), the
circle of a 24x24 image becomes an ellipse 40px wide and 80px tall.

The other engines lay the image out at the size of its box too, going by
their sources. Blink’s SVGImage::DrawForContainer draws the image for
the container size that its SVGImage::DrawInfo carries. WebKit sets that
size with SVGImage::setContainerSize. Gecko’s VectorImage::CreateSurface
hands its viewport size to SVGDocumentWrapper::UpdateViewportBounds.

Run in Chrome 157.0.8103.78, Firefox 151.0, and WebKit 26.5 (the build
that Playwright ships), an img element and a background that doesn’t
repeat both show that ellipse under scale(2, 4). In all three, each one
matches, pixel for pixel, the same image drawn at 48x96 with its
preserveAspectRatio set to none.
